.TH HONE 1 "2026-08-01" "hone 0.1.0" "General Commands Manual"
.SH NAME
hone \- read a run a program judged, write down what it teaches
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hone
.RI [ flags ]
.RI [ session ...]
.br
.B hone forget
.I session-id
.I skill
.RI [ skill ...]
.br
.B hone prompt
.RI [ flags ]
.br
.B hone version
.br
.B hone help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hone
reads an
.BR ask (1)
session in which
.BR ply (1)
worked a goal, and writes down what the run teaches. The lesson is standard
output, progress is standard error, and the exit status says whether
anything was learned.
.PP
It is the fourth of four filters and the smallest of them:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ask     the model      \(em no tools, no loop
brief   the procedure  \(em no model, no loop
ply     the loop       \(em no model, no procedure
hone    the lesson     \(em no store, no retrieval, no format
.fi
.RE
.PP
With no session named, the current conversation. A directory means every
session in it, oldest first, which is what a batch over an archive wants.
Flags come before sessions: after one, a word is a filename.
.SS The rule
.B hone
learns from recoveries, and from nothing else.
.PP
A run that never failed teaches nothing \(em everything worked first try,
so there is no counterfactual. A run that never passed proves nothing \(em
the last thing tried might be wrong, and a lesson drawn from it is a wrong
answer with the standing of a precedent, which is worse than no lesson at
all. Only a run that failed and then passed teaches, and the lesson is the
difference between the two.
.PP
That gate is arithmetic on exit statuses already in the log. A model is used
to
.I word
a lesson and never to decide there is one, because a model asked whether a
transcript contains a lesson will always find one.
.PP
Three runs in four teach nothing. That is the design working, not failing.
.SS The verdict
.B ply \-check
writes how a run ended into the session as an
.B ask note
\(em stamped, and not folded \(em so
.B hone
knows the outcome without guessing. A run with no check has no verdict and
is refused, saying so.
.PP
This is the whole reason it works. Everything else that learns from agent
logs is learning from unlabelled ones, and the difference is not small: in
published comparisons, skills distilled from verified trajectories are worth
roughly an order of magnitude more than skills distilled from unverified
ones.
.SS No store
A lesson is a skill,
.BR brief (1)
is the catalogue, and
.B $BRIEF_PATH
is where it lives. hone writes what brief reads and stops. There is no
index, no database, no embedding, no vector store and no daemon, and
retrieval is
.BR "brief find" .
.PP
Every write is a delta. Lessons are appended under a
.B "## Lessons"
heading and what is already there is never regenerated: a model handed its
own accumulated notes and asked for the next version rewrites them, and
rewriting compresses away the specifics that made them worth keeping.
.SS Provenance
Every lesson carries an HTML comment naming the session it was learned from
and the call that worded it. Both replay, so
.B ask replay \-check
proves either one a year later.
.PP
That is what makes a bad lesson something to delete rather than argue with.
The unit is a run, because a session that taught one wrong thing usually
taught its neighbours too \(em see
.BR "hone forget" .
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B hone \fR[\fIflags\fR] [\fIsession ...\fR]
Distil what a run teaches. Standard output is the lesson, or the path
written when
.B \-into
is given.
.TP
.B hone forget \fIsession-id skill \fR[\fIskill ...\fR]
Remove every lesson a run taught, and the marks that named it. Exit 1 when
there was nothing to forget, which is an answer rather than a failure.
.IP
It takes a session id because that is what a mark records. A skill is a
name brief knows or a path.
.TP
.B hone prompt \fR[\fIflags\fR]
Print the system prompt that words a lesson, on standard output. It is a
value, not a secret, so extending it is ordinary shell.
.B \-n
prints it with the cap it would be sent with.
.TP
.B hone version
Print the version.
.B \-V
and
.B \-\-version
do the same.
.TP
.B hone help
Print the summary on standard output.
.B \-h
and
.B \-\-help
do the same.
.SH FLAGS
.TP
.BI \-into " skill"
Fold the lessons into this skill instead of printing them. A name
.BR brief (1)
knows, or a path.
.B \-into \-
means the skill the run was already following, read back from what
.BR ply (1)
recorded when it loaded it \(em a lesson learned *despite* a procedure
belongs to that procedure. A run that loaded none, or more than one, is
refused rather than guessed at. A skill that does not exist is created, with a
description written for it \(em brief ranks over descriptions and never over
bodies, so a skill described as "what was learned here" is a skill nothing
will ever retrieve.
.TP
.BI \-n " N"
The most lessons to take from one run. Default 3, and small on purpose: a
run that appears to teach five things has usually taught one thing and four
restatements of it.
.TP
.BI \-m " spec"
.IR provider / model
for the wording, e.g.
.BR anthropic/claude\-sonnet\-5 .
Defaults to whatever
.BR ask (1)
would use.
.TP
.B \-N
Say what would be learned and write nothing.
.TP
.B \-why
Print the evidence a lesson would be drawn from, and stop. No model is
called. A lesson is a claim, and reading the evidence before paying for the
claim is the difference between a tool you trust and one you audit
afterwards.
.TP
.BI \-d " dir"
The session directory.
.RB ( $ASK_DIR )
.TP
.B \-no-verify
Skip
.B ask replay \-check
on a session before reading it. The check is on by default because a log
that does not replay is not a record of what happened, and so is not
evidence of anything.
.TP
.B \-q
No progress on standard error.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B ASK
The
.BR ask (1)
binary. Default
.B ask
on
.BR $PATH ,
and required: hone has no model of its own.
.TP
.B BRIEF
The
.BR brief (1)
binary. Default
.B brief
on
.BR $PATH ,
and optional \(em without it hone writes a skill it cannot lint, which is a
narrower hone rather than no hone.
.TP
.B ASK_DIR
Where sessions live. Default
.BR ~/.ask/sessions .
.TP
.B BRIEF_PATH
Where skills live, searched left to right, first match wins. A new skill is
created in the first entry. Default
.BR .claude/skills:~/.claude/skills:~/.brief/skills ,
which is brief's.
.TP
.B HONE_DIR
Where hone keeps the sessions that worded its lessons. Default
.BR ~/.hone/lessons .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Something was learned.
.TP
.B 1
Nothing to learn: no verdict, no recovery, nothing worth keeping, or
already learned from this run.
.TP
.B 2
Error: bad usage, an unreadable session, or
.B ask
failed.
.PP
This is
.BR grep (1)'s
contract rather than
.BR ask (1)'s, because hone asks a question where no is a real answer. Most
runs teach nothing, so a loop over an archive branches on 1 rather than
stopping:
.PP
.RS
.nf
for s in ~/.ask/sessions/*.jsonl; do
    hone \-into house "$s" || continue
done
.fi
.RE
.SH EXAMPLES
What did the last run teach?
.PP
.RS
.nf
hone
.fi
.RE
.PP
Keep it, and use it next time:
.PP
.RS
.nf
hone \-into go\-house
ply \-sh \-s go\-house \-check 'go test ./...' "add a ring buffer"
.fi
.RE
.PP
Learn, then act on what was learned \(em the loop, as a pipeline rather
than a runtime:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ply \-sh \-s "$(brief find "$task")" \-check "$check" "$task"
hone \-into house
.fi
.RE
.PP
Read the evidence before believing a lesson:
.PP
.RS
.nf
hone \-why
.fi
.RE
.PP
That run taught something wrong:
.PP
.RS
.nf
hone forget 20260801\-230441\-4c8100cf68ff97f5 house
.fi
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.B ~/.hone/lessons/
The sessions in which lessons were worded, one per call, kept so that
.B ask replay \-check
can prove any of them.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ask (1),
.BR brief (1),
.BR ply (1)
